# When the Lights Go Out: Teaching farm_soil to Power Itself Back On

*Published in **AgTech Systems & Architecture** | Reading Time: 4 minutes*

---

We introduced **`farm_soil`** as the pure observer of our fleet — it reads soil
moisture and evapotranspiration, reasons about them, and pushes a single daily
digest. It watches; it doesn't act. But there's one small, telling exception, and
it's worth a story because it shows how an observe-only agent earns its *first*
sliver of autonomy without sliding down the slope toward controlling the farm.

## The 2 a.m. failure mode

The irrigation controller `farm_soil` keeps an eye on lives on a Raspberry Pi out
in the field. That Pi is plugged into a cheap **Amazon smart plug**, because we wanted
to be able to remote reset the pi.  Power blips
in rural areas; when one hits, the plug stays off, the Pi stays dark, and the
controller is gone until *a human notices and flips it back on*. Until recently
that's exactly what happened: the watchdog detected the outage and nagged the
operator on Discord — *"reset the Alexa plug"* — and someone, eventually, did.

That's a screen-fatigue problem wearing a different hat. The whole thesis of the
fleet is *you shouldn't have to babysit infrastructure*. So why make a human walk
to an app to push a virtual button?

## The bootstrap paradox

The obvious fix — "have the robot turn its own power back on" — has a trap in it.
The robot that runs *on* the dark Pi can't turn anything on; it isn't running.
You can't pull yourself up by a power cord you're not plugged into.

The resolution is that the recovery doesn't belong to the robot being recovered.
`farm_soil` runs on a **different, always-on machine** and already watches the
field Pi from the outside. It's the natural owner: awake, networked, and
unaffected by the outage it's responding to.

## Using the same controls as a person — again

True to the fleet's core rule, `farm_soil` doesn't get a secret backdoor to the
plug. The Amazon plug has no real API; the only thing that controls it is Alexa.
So the agent does what a person would do — it triggers an **Alexa Routine**,
through a webhook (Voice Monkey), the same routine a human could fire by voice.
The grower's own smart-home account is the only key.

When the watchdog sees the Pi unreachable, it fires the "turn on" trigger, waits
for the Pi to boot, and re-probes. If it comes back — great, the operator gets a
*"self-healed"* note instead of a chore. If a few attempts don't fix it (because
the outage wasn't the plug at all), it falls back to the original Discord nag. The
"turn on" is idempotent: it can never cut power to a Pi that's actually running.

## Why this is still the bottom of the autonomy gradient

It would be easy to call this "actuation" and get nervous. It isn't, in the way
that matters. `farm_soil` still never touches a valve, never moves water, never
makes an agronomic decision. The one thing it can now do is *keep a crashed
computer alive* — an **operational, L0–L1 keep-it-alive action**, bounded,
idempotent, and self-limiting. The gradient is intact; the agent simply stopped
asking a human to do the one thing a human should never have had to do.

That's the quiet lesson: the first responsible step beyond pure observation isn't
controlling the process — it's refusing to let the process go dark on your watch.

